{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# ARTI 403 \u2014 Image Processing Lab 1\n",
        "\n",
        "## Loading, displaying, saving, and representing digital images\n",
        "\n",
        "This notebook completes the tasks in the Student Procedural Manual while explaining each step. It uses:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **OpenCV** to load and save the cameraman image.\n",
        "- **PIL (Pillow)** to load and save `lena_gray_256.tif`.\n",
        "- **Matplotlib** to display images.\n",
        "- **NumPy** to inspect and manipulate image arrays.\n",
        "- **scikit-image** to obtain the cameraman sample from the webpage supplied with the lab.\n",
        "\n",
        "Run the cells from top to bottom. In Cursor or Jupyter, click a cell and press **Shift + Enter**.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Learning outcomes\n",
        "\n",
        "After finishing this notebook, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Explain that a digital image is stored as a NumPy array of pixel values.\n",
        "2. Load images using OpenCV and PIL.\n",
        "3. Display images using Matplotlib.\n",
        "4. Save images to disk.\n",
        "5. Inspect image shape, dimensions, data type, and pixel values.\n",
        "6. Perform basic image operations with NumPy.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 0. Install the required packages\n",
        "\n",
        "You only need to do this once. If the imports in the next cell fail, open Cursor's terminal and run:\n",
        "\n",
        "```powershell\n",
        "python -m pip install numpy matplotlib pillow scikit-image opencv-python jupyter\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "After installation, restart the notebook kernel and continue.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 1. Import the libraries\n",
        "\n",
        "Importing makes the functions from each library available in the notebook.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "from pathlib import Path\n",
        "\n",
        "import cv2\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "from PIL import Image\n",
        "from skimage import data\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Libraries imported successfully.\")\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 2. Prepare the image and results folders\n",
        "\n",
        "The notebook expects this arrangement:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "outputs/\n",
        "\u251c\u2500\u2500 Lab_1_Image_Processing.ipynb\n",
        "\u2514\u2500\u2500 Images/\n",
        "    \u2514\u2500\u2500 lena_gray_256.tif\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The `lab1_results` folder is created automatically. Your original Lena file will not be changed.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "BASE_DIR = Path.cwd()\n",
        "IMAGES_DIR = BASE_DIR / \"Images\"\n",
        "RESULTS_DIR = BASE_DIR / \"lab1_results\"\n",
        "RESULTS_DIR.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "lena_path = IMAGES_DIR / \"lena_gray_256.tif\"\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Notebook folder:\", BASE_DIR)\n",
        "print(\"Images folder:\", IMAGES_DIR)\n",
        "print(\"Results folder:\", RESULTS_DIR)\n",
        "\n",
        "if not lena_path.exists():\n",
        "    raise FileNotFoundError(\n",
        "        f\"Could not find {lena_path}. Put lena_gray_256.tif inside the Images folder.\"\n",
        "    )\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Lena image found:\", lena_path)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 3. Obtain the cameraman image\n",
        "\n",
        "The lab manual refers to `images/cameraman.tif`. The supplied scikit-image webpage provides the same standard cameraman image through `data.camera()`. This cell retrieves the built-in sample and saves it in the `Images` folder so OpenCV can read it from disk.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "IMAGES_DIR.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "cameraman_array = data.camera()\n",
        "cameraman_path = IMAGES_DIR / \"cameraman.tif\"\n",
        "Image.fromarray(cameraman_array).save(cameraman_path)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Cameraman saved to:\", cameraman_path)\n",
        "print(\"Shape:\", cameraman_array.shape)\n",
        "print(\"Data type:\", cameraman_array.dtype)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Task 2A \u2014 Load and visualize an image using OpenCV\n",
        "\n",
        "`cv2.imread()` reads the image file and returns a NumPy array. `cv2.IMREAD_GRAYSCALE` keeps this image as a two-dimensional grayscale array.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "img_cv = cv2.imread(str(cameraman_path), cv2.IMREAD_GRAYSCALE)\n",
        "\n",
        "if img_cv is None:\n",
        "    raise FileNotFoundError(f\"OpenCV could not read {cameraman_path}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(6, 6))\n",
        "plt.imshow(img_cv, cmap=\"gray\", vmin=0, vmax=255)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Cameraman \u2014 loaded with OpenCV\")\n",
        "plt.axis(\"off\")\n",
        "plt.show()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Task 2B \u2014 Load and visualize an image using PIL\n",
        "\n",
        "`Image.open()` returns a PIL Image object. Matplotlib can display that object directly. The grayscale colormap prevents Matplotlib from adding artificial colors.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "img_pil = Image.open(lena_path)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(6, 6))\n",
        "plt.imshow(img_pil, cmap=\"gray\", vmin=0, vmax=255)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Lena \u2014 loaded with PIL\")\n",
        "plt.axis(\"off\")\n",
        "plt.show()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Task 3 \u2014 Store images on disk\n",
        "\n",
        "OpenCV uses `cv2.imwrite()`, while PIL Image objects use `.save()`. These commands create new files inside `lab1_results`.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "opencv_saved_path = RESULTS_DIR / \"cameraman_saved_with_opencv.jpg\"\n",
        "pil_saved_path = RESULTS_DIR / \"lena_saved_with_pil.jpg\"\n",
        "\n",
        "opencv_success = cv2.imwrite(str(opencv_saved_path), img_cv)\n",
        "img_pil.save(pil_saved_path)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"OpenCV save successful:\", opencv_success)\n",
        "print(\"OpenCV output:\", opencv_saved_path)\n",
        "print(\"PIL output:\", pil_saved_path)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Task 4A \u2014 Display the OpenCV image as an array\n",
        "\n",
        "For a grayscale image, the shape is `(height, width)`. Each number is a brightness value:\n",
        "\n",
        "- `0` means black.\n",
        "- `255` means white.\n",
        "- Values between them are shades of gray.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "print(\"Python type:\", type(img_cv))\n",
        "print(\"Shape:\", img_cv.shape)\n",
        "print(\"Number of dimensions:\", img_cv.ndim)\n",
        "print(\"Data type:\", img_cv.dtype)\n",
        "print()\n",
        "print(\"OpenCV image array:\")\n",
        "print(img_cv)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Task 4B \u2014 Convert the PIL image to a NumPy array\n",
        "\n",
        "PIL uses an Image object. `np.array()` converts it into the array representation required for numerical operations.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "img_array = np.array(img_pil)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"PIL object type:\", type(img_pil))\n",
        "print(\"Converted array type:\", type(img_array))\n",
        "print(\"Shape:\", img_array.shape)\n",
        "print(\"Number of dimensions:\", img_array.ndim)\n",
        "print(\"Data type:\", img_array.dtype)\n",
        "print()\n",
        "print(\"Lena image array:\")\n",
        "print(img_array)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Assessment \u2014 Basic NumPy operations\n",
        "\n",
        "The following cells demonstrate typical operations an instructor may ask you to perform on an image array.\n",
        "\n",
        "### A. Image statistics\n",
        "\n",
        "The minimum, maximum, and mean summarize the image's pixel intensities.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "print(\"Minimum pixel value:\", img_array.min())\n",
        "print(\"Maximum pixel value:\", img_array.max())\n",
        "print(\"Mean pixel value:\", round(float(img_array.mean()), 2))\n",
        "print(\"Standard deviation:\", round(float(img_array.std()), 2))\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### B. Access and change one pixel\n",
        "\n",
        "Array indexing uses `[row, column]`. The top-left pixel is at `[0, 0]`.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "row, column = 100, 100\n",
        "print(f\"Pixel at row {row}, column {column}:\", img_array[row, column])\n",
        "\n",
        "pixel_example = img_array.copy()\n",
        "pixel_example[row, column] = 255\n",
        "print(\"The copied pixel was changed to:\", pixel_example[row, column])\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### C. Crop part of the image\n",
        "\n",
        "Slicing selects a range of rows and columns. The ending number is not included.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "cropped = img_array[50:200, 50:200]\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5, 5))\n",
        "plt.imshow(cropped, cmap=\"gray\", vmin=0, vmax=255)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Cropped Lena [50:200, 50:200]\")\n",
        "plt.axis(\"off\")\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Original shape:\", img_array.shape)\n",
        "print(\"Cropped shape:\", cropped.shape)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### D. Create a negative image\n",
        "\n",
        "For an 8-bit image, subtracting each pixel from `255` reverses dark and light areas.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "negative = 255 - img_array\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5, 5))\n",
        "plt.imshow(negative, cmap=\"gray\", vmin=0, vmax=255)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Negative image\")\n",
        "plt.axis(\"off\")\n",
        "plt.show()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### E. Increase brightness\n",
        "\n",
        "Adding `50` makes pixels brighter. Conversion to a larger integer type before addition prevents overflow; `np.clip()` keeps the final values between 0 and 255.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "brighter = np.clip(img_array.astype(np.int16) + 50, 0, 255).astype(np.uint8)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5, 5))\n",
        "plt.imshow(brighter, cmap=\"gray\", vmin=0, vmax=255)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Brightness increased by 50\")\n",
        "plt.axis(\"off\")\n",
        "plt.show()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### F. Flip the image horizontally\n",
        "\n",
        "`[:, ::-1]` keeps all rows and reverses the column order.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "flipped = img_array[:, ::-1]\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5, 5))\n",
        "plt.imshow(flipped, cmap=\"gray\", vmin=0, vmax=255)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Horizontally flipped\")\n",
        "plt.axis(\"off\")\n",
        "plt.show()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### G. Create a binary image using a threshold\n",
        "\n",
        "Pixels above the threshold become white (`255`), and the remaining pixels become black (`0`).\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "threshold_value = 128\n",
        "binary = np.where(img_array > threshold_value, 255, 0).astype(np.uint8)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(5, 5))\n",
        "plt.imshow(binary, cmap=\"gray\", vmin=0, vmax=255)\n",
        "plt.title(f\"Binary image \u2014 threshold = {threshold_value}\")\n",
        "plt.axis(\"off\")\n",
        "plt.show()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Compare all NumPy results\n",
        "\n",
        "This figure makes the effect of each operation easy to compare.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 3, figsize=(12, 8))\n",
        "\n",
        "results = [\n",
        "    (img_array, \"Original Lena\"),\n",
        "    (cropped, \"Cropped\"),\n",
        "    (negative, \"Negative\"),\n",
        "    (brighter, \"Brighter\"),\n",
        "    (flipped, \"Flipped\"),\n",
        "    (binary, \"Binary\"),\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "for axis, (image, title) in zip(axes.flat, results):\n",
        "    axis.imshow(image, cmap=\"gray\", vmin=0, vmax=255)\n",
        "    axis.set_title(title)\n",
        "    axis.axis(\"off\")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Save the NumPy results\n",
        "\n",
        "This cell saves each processed array as an image and saves the comparison figure.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "Image.fromarray(cropped).save(RESULTS_DIR / \"lena_cropped.png\")\n",
        "Image.fromarray(negative).save(RESULTS_DIR / \"lena_negative.png\")\n",
        "Image.fromarray(brighter).save(RESULTS_DIR / \"lena_brighter.png\")\n",
        "Image.fromarray(flipped).save(RESULTS_DIR / \"lena_flipped.png\")\n",
        "Image.fromarray(binary).save(RESULTS_DIR / \"lena_binary.png\")\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.savefig(RESULTS_DIR / \"numpy_operations_comparison.png\", dpi=150, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"All result images were saved in:\", RESULTS_DIR)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Conclusion\n",
        "\n",
        "In this lab:\n",
        "\n",
        "- OpenCV and PIL loaded images from disk.\n",
        "- Matplotlib displayed the images.\n",
        "- OpenCV and PIL saved new image files.\n",
        "- NumPy represented each grayscale image as a two-dimensional array.\n",
        "- Each array element stored one pixel intensity from 0 to 255.\n",
        "- NumPy slicing and arithmetic produced crops, negatives, brightness changes, flips, and binary images.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Questions to check your understanding\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Why does a grayscale image have two dimensions?\n",
        "2. What do the values `0` and `255` represent?\n",
        "3. What is the difference between a PIL Image object and a NumPy array?\n",
        "4. Why do we use `np.clip()` when increasing brightness?\n",
        "5. What happens when you change the binary threshold from `128` to `80` or `180`?\n"
      ]
    }
  ],
